{"schema":"zzx-html-edition-profile-v1","country":"VG","name":"British Virgin Islands","year":2007,"status":"source-extracted; field review pending","source":{"name":"factbook-2007.zip","member":"factbook-2007/geos/vi.html","sha256":"4d744cf5ec2bb9f728c62f35ea4920bce8fe82e781a545299e07e136597757f3","format":"html"},"rights_notice_member":"factbook-2007/docs/contributor_copyright.html","rights_notice_sha256":"0aa0c111c3015a738f223e5be4ed3275a829514a52d1781742a8671e915bdb1e","fields":[{"category":"introduction","label":"Background","content":"First inhabited by Arawak and later by Carib Indians, the Virgin Islands were settled by the Dutch in 1648 and then annexed by the English in 1672. The islands were part of the British colony of the Leeward Islands from 1872-1960; they were granted autonomy in 1967. The economy is closely tied to the larger and more populous US Virgin Islands to the west; the US dollar is the legal currency.","ordinal":1,"locator":"factbook-2007/geos/vi.html#field:1","country":"VG","edition_year":2007},{"category":"geography","label":"Location","content":"Caribbean, between the Caribbean Sea and the North Atlantic Ocean, east of Puerto Rico","ordinal":2,"locator":"factbook-2007/geos/vi.html#field:2","country":"VG","edition_year":2007},{"category":"geography","label":"Geographic coordinates","content":"18 30 N, 64 30 W","ordinal":3,"locator":"factbook-2007/geos/vi.html#field:3","country":"VG","edition_year":2007},{"category":"geography","label":"Map references","content":"Central America and the Caribbean","ordinal":4,"locator":"factbook-2007/geos/vi.html#field:4","country":"VG","edition_year":2007},{"category":"geography","label":"Area","content":"total: 153 sq km\n\nland: 153 sq km\n\nwater: 0 sq km\n\nnote: comprised of 16 inhabited and more than 20 uninhabited islands; includes the islands of Tortola, Anegada, Virgin Gorda, Jost van Dyke","ordinal":5,"locator":"factbook-2007/geos/vi.html#field:5","country":"VG","edition_year":2007},{"category"